{
  "dom_id": "step-q343662",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+y=-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?",
  "worked_solution": "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,\u27f9\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =1.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
